Dialogue History:
Assistant: What are the primary outcomes Tarrant County wants this integrated justice capability to achieve for indigent defense services, and how will success be measured?
User: The main outcome is to make appointed-counsel processing faster, more consistent, and less manual so the Office of Attorney Appointments can handle requests with better information and fewer delays. We also want smoother coordination across the criminal justice community, with timely notifications and better sharing of defendant and case details so appointments are made accurately.
Success would probably be measured by reduced turnaround time from request to appointment, fewer data entry errors or missing information, and fewer follow-up calls or emails to chase down case details. We’d also look at whether users can see the status of a request more easily and whether notifications are getting the right people at the right time, but the exact metrics would still need to be defined with the team.
Assistant: Which stakeholder groups or user roles will interact with this integrated justice capability, and what responsibilities or permissions should each role have?
User: The main users will be staff in the Office of Attorney Appointments, since they’ll be receiving and processing the requests and coordinating the appointment workflow. We’ll also have judges or court staff submitting or initiating requests, and attorneys who may need appointment information and notifications. On the justice-system side, there will likely be jail, clerk, prosecutor, and maybe public defender or other court-connected staff who provide or consume case information, though the exact role permissions still need to be worked out.
In general, Office staff should be able to review cases, verify eligibility information, assign or route appointments, and send notifications. Court users should probably be able to create or submit requests and see status updates relevant to their cases, while attorneys should receive appointment details and updates but not edit case records. Other agencies would likely have more limited access, mostly to share or view the specific information they’re responsible for.
Assistant: What are the key business rules that should govern how appointed-counsel requests are reviewed, prioritized, approved or rejected, assigned to attorneys, and routed for notifications—for example, eligibility checks, exceptions, deadlines, or criteria that determine who can act on a request and when?
User: A request should not move forward until the core defendant and case information is complete enough to make a decision, and the office needs to be able to verify that the person actually qualifies for appointed counsel under county rules. If something is missing or the case is in an exception category, staff should be able to hold it, reject it, or route it for manual review instead of forcing an automatic approval.
Priority would usually depend on case type, court requirements, and any deadlines already attached to the matter, so urgent hearings or expiring timelines should surface first. Only authorized Office of Attorney Appointments staff should approve or assign counsel, while courts may initiate requests and other agencies may provide supporting information but not make the appointment decision. Notifications should go out when a request is received, when it is approved or rejected, when counsel is assigned, and when something changes that affects the parties involved, but I’d want to verify the exact trigger points and exception handling with the team.
Assistant: What specific types of requests or case situations should be treated as exception categories, and for each one, what should the system do—such as hold, reject, or route to manual review?
User: The most obvious exceptions would be requests with missing identity or case details, duplicate requests, conflicts of interest, or situations where the person is clearly not eligible based on the information available. Those should usually be held or routed to manual review rather than automatically approved, because staff need a chance to verify what’s wrong and correct it if possible.
We’d also treat special case types, like emergencies, late-night or same-day requests, and maybe cases involving minors, detention status changes, or unusual court orders, as manual-review items if they don’t fit the normal workflow. If the issue is a hard disqualifier, then rejection makes sense; if it’s just incomplete or ambiguous, holding the request and sending it to the right staff is better. The exact exception list and action for each one would still need to be defined with the office and the courts.

